script:
{
  "topic": "Living in a car after a sudden job loss",
  "title": "POV: You Have a Full-Time Job and You Live In Your Car",
  "sections": [
    {
      "name": "Level 1: The Catalyst",
      "segments": [
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: First-person POV of YOUR hands gripping a steering wheel, parked at night in a desolate industrial park. The only light is from your phone screen, illuminating your knuckles. You are a tired man in his 30s with a grey hoodie.",
          "narration": "The engine has been off for an hour, but you haven't moved. The cold is starting to seep through the floorboards of your 2011 Honda Civic. You look from the steering wheel to the passenger seat, then the back. Six years of your life fits into one cardboard box, a gym bag, and three garbage sacks. A coffee mug, a phone charger, a framed photo from a fishing trip, and a stapler you're pretty sure was never yours.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: Extreme close-up on a smartphone screen. A banking app is open, showing a balance of $743.12. The text is sharp and brutally clear.",
          "narration": "You open the banking app for the tenth time, as if the number will change. $743.12. Your last paycheck. Rent is $1,450 and it was due nine days ago. The security deposit is a ghost. You do the math again, knowing it's pointless. It doesn't add up. It will never add up again. The eviction notice wasn't a surprise, but its finality feels like a physical blow. Your key card stopped working at the office two weeks ago. Now your apartment key is useless too.",
          "pause": true,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: First-person POV, looking at your own reflection in the car's dark side mirror. Your face is barely visible, just a silhouette against the dim, lonely lights of the industrial park.",
          "narration": "You tell yourself it's temporary. A stopgap. A few weeks to get back on your feet. You're not homeless. People who are homeless sleep on the street, under bridges. You have a car. You have a gym membership for showers. You have a job... or you will, soon. You just need a break. This is the first lie you tell yourself. It will not be the last.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Level 2: The Descent",
      "segments": [
        {
          "type": "graphic",
          "visual": "graphic: Storage Unit Bill: $120/month. Car Insurance: $80/month. Gas: $300/month (est).",
          "narration": "The first week is about logistics. The math of survival. Your life is now a spreadsheet of small, bleeding costs. The biggest is the 10x10 storage unit. $120 a month to house the ghost of your old life. Your furniture, your books, your winter coat. You tell yourself you'll get it all back soon. It's an investment. Car insurance is $80. Gas is suddenly a critical expense, not for commuting, but for charging your phone and running the heat until the engine block cools.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: POV shot of YOUR hands carefully folding a blanket in the cramped back seat of the car. You're trying to create a level surface to sleep on, but it's awkward and uneven.",
          "narration": "Sleep doesn't come. Not real sleep. It's a series of two-hour naps, punctuated by the sound of a passing truck or the glare of headlights. You learn to park in the darkest corner of a 24-hour Walmart lot, far from the entrance, but not so far you look suspicious. You learn the rhythm of the overnight shift, the street sweepers, the security patrol. Every knock on the window, real or imagined, sends a jolt of adrenaline through your chest.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A tired man in his 30s with a grey hoodie, seen from behind, brushing his teeth in the sterile, brightly-lit bathroom of a 24-hour gym.",
          "narration": "The gym is your sanctuary and your shame. A Planet Fitness membership is $24.99 a month for showers, clean water, and a place to exist for an hour without being questioned. You walk in with your gym bag, trying to look like you belong. Like you just finished a workout, not like you just spent eight hours trying to sleep in a metal box. You avoid eye contact, get in, get clean, get out. The locker room mirror shows a stranger looking back at you.",
          "pause": true,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: Close-up on a single gas station tuna salad sandwich, sitting on the passenger seat. The packaging is cheap and crinkled.",
          "narration": "Food is a new challenge. No fridge, no microwave. Your diet becomes a rotation of gas station sandwiches, protein bars, and bags of chips. Anything that can be eaten cold, in your car, in two minutes. You buy a single banana for breakfast because it feels like a healthy choice, a small act of normalcy in a life that is anything but. The smell of stale coffee and processed food begins to cling to the car's upholstery.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Level 3: The Unspoken Rules",
      "segments": [
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: First-person POV, looking through the windshield. A police car is slowly cruising through the parking lot, its spotlight sweeping across the parked cars. Your hands are frozen on the wheel.",
          "narration": "You learn the first rule of this life on night seventeen. The Knock. It's not a polite tap. It's two sharp raps from a flashlight on your driver's side window. A police officer. He doesn't ask if you're okay. He asks what you're doing here. He tells you about the city ordinance against 'vehicular vagrancy'. Loitering. He tells you to move along. It's not a suggestion. It's a command that carries the unspoken threat of a ticket, an impound, the end of the line.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "graphic",
          "visual": "graphic: RULE #1: Never park in the same spot twice.",
          "narration": "So you learn the rules. Rule #1: Never park in the same spot twice in a week. You build a mental map of the city's blind spots. Industrial parks after 6 PM. The back rows of hotel parking lots. The visitor section of a hospital garage. You become a ghost, a flicker in the city's peripheral vision. Your car, once a symbol of freedom, is now your cage and your camouflage.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A tired man in his 30s with a grey hoodie sits in the driver's seat, applying for jobs on a laptop balanced precariously on his knees. The screen's glow is the only light.",
          "narration": "The job hunt is a special kind of hell. You spend your days at the public library, using their Wi-Fi, pretending to be a freelancer. You need an address for the applications. You use your old one, praying they don't send anything by mail. You get a call for an interview. A logistics manager position. It pays well. For a moment, you feel a spark of hope. Then comes the question: 'Can you hop on a video call this afternoon?'",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: Close up on YOUR face, looking into the laptop's camera. You've propped your phone up to provide better lighting, and you're wearing a clean shirt, but the background is the cramped, cluttered interior of your car.",
          "narration": "You do the interview in your car, parked in a quiet suburban neighborhood. You put on a clean shirt you keep for this purpose. You angle the laptop just right so they can't see the condensation on the windows or the sleeping bag bunched up in the back. You smile, you talk about synergy and KPIs. You feel like a complete fraud. The interviewer says they'll be in touch. They never are.",
          "pause": true,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "graphic",
          "visual": "graphic: RULE #2: Self-pity is a luxury you cannot afford.",
          "narration": "This is where Rule #2 kicks in: Self-pity is the only thing out here that will actually kill you. Despair is a weight that will sink you. You can't afford it. You have to treat this like a job. The job is survival. 8 AM, wake up. 9 AM, gym. 10 AM to 5 PM, library, job applications. 6 PM, find a parking spot. 10 PM, try to sleep. The routine is the only thing that keeps the panic at bay.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: POV shot of YOUR hands using a public library computer. You're not looking at jobs, but at a map of 'Safe Parking' locations in a nearby city.",
          "narration": "You start hearing whispers online. Reddit threads, forums for 'urban campers'. They talk about 'safe parking programs'. Designated lots, run by non-profits, where you can park overnight without being harassed. There's one in a city 50 miles away. It's a lottery system. There's a waitlist. But it's a real plan. A destination. For the first time in a month, you have something to move toward.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A tired man in his 30s with a grey hoodie is on the phone, leaning against his car. His expression is a mix of desperation and forced politeness.",
          "narration": "You call them. A woman with a kind, tired voice explains the process. You need a valid driver's license, registration, and insurance. You need to pass a background check. You need to be actively looking for work or housing. 'We have about 200 clients,' she says. 'The list is long, but don't give up.' You hang up, and the simple act of being spoken to with dignity almost makes you break down.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "graphic",
          "visual": "graphic: RULE #3: Always park facing the exit.",
          "narration": "That night, you add Rule #3 to your list: Always park facing the exit. It's a small thing, a habit you picked up from a post online. It saves you a few seconds if you need to leave in a hurry. It's about maintaining the illusion of control, the feeling that you are ready, that you are not trapped. It's a lie, but it's a useful one.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: Close-up on the car's dashboard. The 'Check Engine' light is on, a stark, ominous orange glow in the dark interior.",
          "narration": "Then, a new disaster. A grinding noise from the engine, followed by a shudder. The 'Check Engine' light blinks on. You know, with a certainty that makes your stomach turn to ice, that this is not a small problem. Your car isn't just your home; it's your only asset. And it's dying.",
          "pause": true,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A greasy, handwritten estimate from a mechanic. The total at the bottom reads: '$1,850 - Transmission Repair'. YOUR thumb is next to the number, smudged with dirt.",
          "narration": "A mechanic in a greasy jumpsuit confirms your fears the next day. The transmission. He quotes you $1,850. He might as well have said a million. You have $412 to your name. You thank him, your voice hollow, and drive away. The grinding sound is the soundtrack to your new reality. Your lifeline is breaking.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Level 4: The Bottom & The Pivot",
      "segments": [
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A tired man in his 30s with a grey hoodie stands in a line of men outside a day labor office at dawn. The light is grey and cold. Everyone stares straight ahead.",
          "narration": "The dying car changes the math. You can't afford to wait for a salaried job anymore. You need cash. Now. You find a day labor office. You're there at 5 AM, standing in a line with two dozen other men. Men with scarred hands and tired eyes. No one talks. The unspoken rule here is simple: you are a body, available for a price.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: POV shot of YOUR hands, now covered in dirt and blisters, holding a shovel. You're on a construction site, moving gravel.",
          "narration": "Your name gets called. Landscaping. $15 an hour, cash at the end of the day. For ten hours, you haul mulch and dig trenches under a relentless sun. Your office-soft hands are raw within the first hour. Your back screams in protest. But at 6 PM, a man puts eight twenty-dollar bills and a ten into your hand. $170. It feels like a fortune.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: Close-up of a hand-drawn budget in a small notebook. Lines for 'Transmission Fund', 'Gas', 'Food'. A small amount, '$50', is written next to 'Transmission Fund'.",
          "narration": "This becomes your new routine. Up at 4:30 AM. Hope for a job. Work until you can't feel your arms. You eat the cheapest food possible: ramen, bread, peanut butter. Every dollar is triaged. Gas. Food. Phone bill. The rest goes into an envelope tucked under your seat, labeled 'Transmission'. It's a slow, brutal climb. $50. $120. $250.",
          "pause": true,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A tired man in his 30s with a grey hoodie is sitting in his car at night, eating cold ramen from a cup. He looks utterly exhausted, but also determined.",
          "narration": "Something shifts in you. The shame is still there, a constant companion. But it's joined by a grim sense of pride. You are surviving this. You are not broken. You are earning your right to exist, one dollar at a time. The despair that was threatening to drown you is replaced by a cold, hard focus. The focus of a man with nothing left to lose.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A calendar on a phone screen. A date is circled two months in the future. The note says 'CAR REPAIR'.",
          "narration": "It takes you 52 days of back-breaking labor to save the money. 52 days of waking up in the cold, of washing your face in a gas station bathroom, of ignoring the ache in your bones. On the 53rd day, you drive your rattling car back to the mechanic's shop. You hand him a thick, crumpled envelope of cash. You don't bargain. You just pay.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: First-person POV from the driver's seat. Both YOUR hands are on the steering wheel as you drive smoothly down a highway. The 'Check Engine' light is off. The engine sounds quiet and healthy.",
          "narration": "When you pick up the car, the engine is quiet. The grinding is gone. You drive it onto the highway and push it to 65. It's smooth. Stable. For the first time in months, you feel like you're moving forward, not just running from something. You've hit the bottom, and you've started to dig your way out.",
          "pause": true,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A smartphone screen displaying an email. The subject line reads: 'Update: Your Application for Safe Parking LA'.",
          "narration": "As you're driving, your phone buzzes. An email. From the safe parking program. You pull over, your heart pounding. You'd almost forgotten about it. 'Dear Applicant,' it begins. 'We have a spot available for you, starting next week.'",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A tired man in his 30s with a grey hoodie leans his head against the steering wheel, his eyes closed. It's not clear if he's crying or just overwhelmed with relief.",
          "narration": "You read the email three times. A spot. A legal place to park. A place with a security guard and a portable toilet. A place where you won't get The Knock. You lean your head against the steering wheel and the tension you've been holding in your shoulders for months finally begins to release. It's not a home. But it's a start.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: POV shot looking out the windshield at a numbered parking spot in a fenced-in lot at night. Other cars are visible, people quietly getting ready for 'bed'.",
          "narration": "The safe parking lot is not what you expected. It's quiet, orderly. There are rules. In by 9 PM, out by 7 AM. No loud music. No visitors. But there's a sense of community. A shared understanding. You see a woman in her 60s reading a book in her van. A young couple quietly making sandwiches. These are your neighbors now.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A case manager, a kind-looking woman in her 40s, sits across a folding table from you. She's pointing at a resource sheet.",
          "narration": "The program comes with a case manager. Her name is Maria. She doesn't just check on your parking status; she helps you get a mailing address through their office. She reviews your resume. She tells you about a local company hiring warehouse supervisors, a job that uses your logistics background. She sees you not as a problem to be solved, but as a person who needs a bridge.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Level 5: The Escalation",
      "segments": [
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A tired man in his 30s with a grey hoodie is standing in front of a mirror, adjusting the collar on a new, clean work shirt. He looks nervous but professional.",
          "narration": "You get the interview for the warehouse supervisor job. You use the last of your savings to buy a new shirt and get a haircut. You walk into the interview, and this time, you're not a fraud. You're a man who has spent the last six months living a life of extreme logistical challenges. You know how to manage inventory because you've managed your own survival down to the last dollar.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: Close up on a handshake between you (a tired man in his 30s with a grey hoodie) and a hiring manager in a polished office environment.",
          "narration": "You tell them about your experience. You don't tell them you've been living in your car, but you talk about problem-solving, resilience, and managing under pressure. You speak with the authority of someone who has lived it. Two days later, they offer you the job. $26 an hour. Full-time. With benefits.",
          "pause": true,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: POV shot of YOUR hands holding your first paycheck. The numbers are clear and crisp. You can see your name printed on it.",
          "narration": "Your first paycheck feels like a fantasy. After taxes, it's more money than you've seen in one place for almost a year. You don't celebrate. You don't splurge. You sit in your car, in the safe parking lot, and you make a plan. The discipline you learned at rock bottom is now your most valuable tool.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A spreadsheet is open on a laptop. Columns for 'Income', 'Savings', 'Rent Fund', 'Debt'. The numbers are slowly filling in.",
          "narration": "You keep living in the car. For another three months. The temptation to rent the first cheap room you find is immense. But Maria, your case manager, advised you to wait. To build a buffer. So you work. You save every penny. You watch your bank account grow from three digits to four, then five. You are building a wall between you and the abyss.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A tired man in his 30s with a grey hoodie stands in an empty, clean, but small apartment. Sunlight is streaming through the window. He's holding a single key.",
          "narration": "Finally, the day comes. You find a small studio apartment. It's not fancy. The paint is a little chipped. But it's clean, it's safe, and it's yours. You sign the lease, pay the first month's rent and the security deposit in full. The landlord hands you the key. You stand in the empty room, and the silence is overwhelming.",
          "pause": true,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: You (a tired man in his 30s with a grey hoodie) are lying on your back on the carpeted floor of your new, empty apartment. You're not sleeping, just staring at the ceiling with a small, exhausted smile.",
          "narration": "That first night, you don't even have a bed. You sleep on the floor, using your jacket as a pillow. It's the best sleep you've had in a year. No fear of The Knock. No engine block cooling. No cramped back seat. Just the quiet hum of a refrigerator and the profound, unbelievable luxury of a ceiling above your head.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: Close-up on a smartphone. You are transferring money from your checking account to your storage unit's payment portal. The screen reads 'Final Payment & Move Out'.",
          "narration": "A week later, you go to the storage unit. You pay the final bill. You and a friend from work load your old life into a U-Haul. The furniture looks a little worn, the boxes are dusty. It feels like unearthing a time capsule from a different person's life. A person who took things like a couch and a bed for granted.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "twist",
          "visual": "broll: A tired man in his 30s with a grey hoodie is at his new warehouse job, looking at a clipboard. In the background, a new employee, a young man looking nervous and lost, is starting his first day.",
          "narration": "Your new job is demanding, but you're good at it. You see the inefficiencies, the waste, the places where the system breaks down. You see it because you've lived it. One day, your boss asks you to help train a new hire. A young kid, maybe 22. He's quiet, hardworking, but you notice his shoes are falling apart and he never eats lunch.",
          "pause": true,
          "twist": true
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: From your over-the-shoulder perspective, you watch the young new hire get into a battered-looking old car in the parking lot after work. He's putting up sunshades in the windows, even though it's evening.",
          "narration": "You watch him after work one day. He doesn't drive off. He moves his car to the far corner of the employee lot. You see him put up sunshades in the windows. It's a gesture you know intimately. It's the gesture of someone trying to build a wall of privacy where none exists. You realize he's living in his car. Right here, in the company parking lot.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: You (a tired man in his 30s with a grey hoodie) approach the young man's car. You're not angry or accusatory. You're holding two bottles of water. Your expression is one of understanding.",
          "narration": "The old you would have ignored it. The you from a year ago would have been too wrapped in his own misery to notice. The current you walks over, taps gently on the glass - a soft tap, not The Knock - and offers him a bottle of water. You don't ask him what he's doing. You don't offer pity. You just say, 'Hey. I'm Mark. If you ever need anything, just let me know.'",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Level 6: The Reversal",
      "segments": [
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A tired man in his 30s with a grey hoodie sits across from his boss in a modern office. He's not asking for a raise, he's presenting a proposal.",
          "narration": "You start to see the problem everywhere. The kid in the parking lot isn't the only one. You see the signs in others. The same worn-out clothes. The people who always have an excuse for why they can't join after-work drinks. You go to your boss with a proposal. Not about warehouse efficiency, but about human efficiency.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "graphic",
          "visual": "graphic: Proposal: 'Employee Safe Parking Initiative'.",
          "narration": "You call it the 'Employee Safe Parking Initiative'. You use the company's own language. 'This isn't charity,' you argue. 'It's risk mitigation. It's employee retention. A worker who is worried about where they're going to sleep is a worker who makes mistakes. A worker who gets sick. A worker who quits.'",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A detailed diagram showing a small, unused section of the company's massive parking lot, re-designated with numbered spots, a portable toilet, and a small security kiosk.",
          "narration": "You map it out. A designated, secure corner of the massive employee parking lot. A simple agreement with a portable toilet company. A partnership with a local credit union to offer financial literacy workshops. You use the data from the non-profit that helped you. You show the costs, the benefits, the ROI. You speak the language of the system.",
          "pause": true,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: The boss, a professional-looking man in his 50s, leans back in his chair, looking at your proposal. He's not dismissing it. He's thinking.",
          "narration": "Your boss is skeptical. But he's also a pragmatist. He's seen the turnover rates. He knows the cost of hiring and training new people. He listens. He asks hard questions. You have the answers. Not because you're a genius, but because you've lived the problem. You know exactly what's needed because you know what you needed.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A company-wide email is on a computer screen. Subject: 'Announcing a Pilot Program: Secure Overnight Parking'.",
          "narration": "A month later, a company-wide email goes out. They're launching a pilot program. It's a watered-down version of your proposal, but it's real. They've allocated ten spots. They're putting you in charge of coordinating it, a special project on top of your supervisor duties. You've gone from being a 'vehicular vagrant' to running a private safe parking program.",
          "pause": true,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: You (a tired man in his 30s with a grey hoodie) are standing in the designated parking area, talking to the young new hire. You're handing him a welcome packet with rules and resource numbers.",
          "narration": "The first person you sign up is the kid, Alex. You hand him a small packet with the rules, a list of resources, and Maria's number at the non-profit. He looks at you, his eyes filled with a relief so profound it's almost painful to see. 'How did you know?' he asks quietly. You just shake your head. 'It takes one to know one,' you say.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: You are on the phone, but this time you're the one offering help. You're talking to a local food bank organizer, arranging for weekly deliveries.",
          "narration": "Running the program is a new kind of education. You're not just a supervisor anymore. You're a liaison, a counselor, a resource. You learn about food banks, about low-cost legal aid, about mental health services. You are building the safety net you never had. You are becoming the person you needed when you were at your worst.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A tired man in his 30s with a grey hoodie looks out his office window at the small, designated parking area. A few cars are pulling in for the night. The sun is setting.",
          "narration": "But there's a new weight. The trap of the solution. The program is a success. Too much of a success. The ten spots are always full. There's a waitlist. You get emails from employees at other companies asking how they can do the same thing. You realize you haven't solved the problem. You've just created a more humane waiting room.",
          "pause": true,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: Close up on a stack of applications for the safe parking program. Names, employee IDs, vehicle types. More applications than you have spots.",
          "narration": "You now have the power to grant a small measure of stability. But you also have the power to deny it. You have to decide who gets a spot and who has to keep risking The Knock. You are now a gatekeeper in the very system that almost crushed you. It's a promotion that feels like a punishment.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
